WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.515
First Nations Water Crisis.

00:00:02.465 --> 00:00:09.818
In a G7 nation with the third largest freshwater reserves on Earth, dozens of Indigenous communities lack safe drinking water.

00:00:09.818 --> 00:00:14.022
The government promised to end all long-term boil water advisories.

00:00:14.022 --> 00:00:16.148
Billions were allocated.

00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:18.295
Advisories persist.

00:00:18.295 --> 00:00:27.909
The water crisis is the clearest measure of institutional priorities: a government that spent ninety-three million on a COVID questionnaire cannot deliver clean water to its own citizens.

00:00:27.909 --> 00:00:30.238
The promise versus reality.

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:38.454
In 2015, the government promised to end all long-term drinking water advisories on First Nations reserves by March 2021.

00:00:38.454 --> 00:00:42.954
Eight point two billion dollars has been allocated since 2016.

00:00:42.954 --> 00:00:46.465
One hundred and forty-three advisories have been lifted.

00:00:46.465 --> 00:00:51.204
But twenty-eight or more long-term advisories remain as of 2026.

00:00:51.204 --> 00:00:55.738
Some communities have been under boil water advisories for over twenty years.

00:00:55.738 --> 00:00:58.943
The target was missed by five years and counting.

00:00:58.943 --> 00:01:01.659
The infrastructure priority question.

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:06.443
The government spent ninety-three million on ArriveCAN, a COVID border questionnaire.

00:01:06.443 --> 00:01:11.670
It spent nine point three billion on Phoenix Pay, a pay system that still doesn't work.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:15.806
It allocated thirty-five billion to the Canada Infrastructure Bank.

00:01:15.806 --> 00:01:20.249
But it cannot deliver clean drinking water to twenty-eight Indigenous communities.

00:01:20.249 --> 00:01:22.954
The water crisis is not about money.

00:01:22.954 --> 00:01:27.136
It is about what the institutional architecture chooses to prioritize.
